-# Concurrent rollout outbox
-Repair controller.py for an offline, durable deployment controller with dependency-DAG plans and a three-command window. Dispatch reserves resources but is not observed success. Receipts can arrive in any order, fail independently, be duplicated, or arrive after a newer epoch. The supplied production plan combines replica replacement and scale-out across two zones, with delayed and independently failed receipts. data/input.json includes configuration, an install payload, and a trace of subsequent apply requests; exercise those requests in listed order. Some requests may legitimately reject under the rules below: a rejection leaves the version and all persisted state unchanged, and later requests must still be processed. The file supplies no expected outcomes.
+# Contingent rollout outbox
+Repair `controller.py`: a durable offline controller must complete a fenced rollout despite **at most one definitive replacement-start failure per epoch**, without losing any service's ready instance. A safe all-success schedule alone is insufficient. Existing starts may be in flight; a successful readiness acknowledgment does not free the old source. Implement the specified reusable planner and persistence API, exercise `data/input.json`, and add regression tests.
 
-## Persistent API
-initialize(path,configuration), inspect(path), apply(path,request), with SQLite path. configuration={nodes:{node:{rack,zone,capacity}},rack_capacity:{rack:integer},services:{service:{size,min_ready,min_zones}},initial:{node:null|{service,image}},max_inflight:1..3}. All nodes always appear in an inventory; each holds at most one unit. initial must be safe. Equal initialize is idempotent; different configuration rejects. inspect returns {version,epoch,observed,pending,done,target}. Initially version=epoch=0, observed=initial,pending={},done=[],target=null.
+This is a fictional higher-level orchestration policy inspired by deployment readiness and stalled-rollout remediation. Fixed one-time cutover tickets, node quarantine, and the stated fault allowance are this workflow's explicit operational rules, not Kubernetes defaults. Each ticket authorizes exactly one SUCCESSFUL cutover of its service this epoch. A failed start may be retried on another authorized destination; relocating an already-cut-over service, restoring old images, or inventing additional tickets is outside this plan.
 
-All apply requests include id,expected_version,kind. Exact structural-JSON replay (including expected_version) returns the saved original response BEFORE CAS, even after restart/newer requests. Conflicting ID reuse raises ValueError. Every new accepted request increments version once, including no-work dispatch/stale receipt/no-op receipt. Every failure raises ValueError and leaves ALL persisted state unchanged. Calls must serialize across concurrent processes; inputs must not mutate. Responses are inspect summary plus kind-specific fields. Exact error strings are irrelevant.
+## API and saved state
+`initialize(path, configuration)`, `inspect(path)`, `apply(path, request)` use a SQLite file. Configuration is `{nodes:[node IDs], capacity:int, max_handoffs:1|2, initial:{node:null|{service,image}}}`. Node IDs in nodes are distinct. Every node has unit capacity. `capacity` is a global bound on present instances plus reserved starts. Initially each service has exactly one ready instance. All nodes appear in the inventory. An equal initialize is idempotent; a different configuration raises ValueError. No API mutates its inputs.
 
-## Install
-{kind:'install',epoch,observed,actions,target,...}. New epoch must be strictly larger. observed is an authoritative freshly fenced inventory; it supersedes old in-flight commands, whose eventual receipts must be stale. target is the exact desired inventory. actions is a DAG (<=8): {id,depends:[action IDs],op:'start'|'stop',node,service,image}; unique IDs, known references/node/service, no self edge. start requires empty node and installs exact identity; stop requires matching existing service/image and empties it. Both observed and target must be safe. Accept only if there exists a dependency-respecting all-success sequential execution of ALL actions, safe after each action, ending exactly at target. Cycles, impossible preconditions or wrong terminal target reject. A greedy first action need not find a feasible schedule. Installing resets done/pending/attempt counters/settled receipts for this epoch, but global request replay persists. Return installed:true.
+Every response contains at least these fields. Additional metadata fields are permitted on fixed-field response, command, handoff, instance and ticket records. Identity maps must have exactly the specified node/token/service entries, and lists have the specified members/order; metadata does not authorize extra map entries. All documented fields retain their defined meaning:
+- `version`, `epoch`: integers, initially zero.
+- `observed`: node→null or `{service,image}` for acknowledged ready instances. During a handoff the old and new instance may both be present.
+- `pending`: token→`{op:'start'|'retire', service, source, target, image, attempt}`. `image` is the NEW image for starts and OLD image for retirements. These are complete command objects, not action-ID strings.
+- `handoffs`: service→`{source,target,phase:'starting'|'ready'|'retiring',attempt}`. Starting has an unknown start receipt; ready has a successful start but no retirement dispatched; retiring has its old-source retirement pending. A successful start commits this handoff; its old source must then retire.
+- `done`: sorted service-ID strings whose retirements have been acknowledged.
+- `quarantine`: sorted node IDs; these nodes are empty but unavailable for new starts this epoch and consume no quota.
+- `faults_left`: remaining start failures allowed, initially zero.
+- `rollouts`: initially null, then service→the installed ticket; targets are sorted in this summary.
+- `complete`: false before install; afterward true exactly when every installed service is done.
 
-## Safety and dispatch
